<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rassemblement Démocratique Africain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as African Democratic Rally, RDA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rassemblement Démocratique Africain was an inter-territorial political movement founded at a congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> in October 1946 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Félix Houphouët-Boigny</a>. It had branches in most territories of French West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">Equatorial Africa</a> and campaigned for African rights within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>. Its sections, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and the Sudanese Union, led several countries to independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> of 1946 gave the colonies of French Africa wider citizenship and the right to elect members of the French National Assembly. Félix Houphouët-Boigny of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> persuaded Lamine Guèye of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, the leading African socialist, to work for a wider movement seeking autonomy, and African deputies signed a manifesto calling a meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a>. The French Socialist leadership, seeing a threat to its own ties, made its African members withdraw, so the new party was backed only by the French Communist Party, with which it sat in coalition in the Assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the elections of November 1946 the RDA won 10 seats from French West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">Equatorial Africa</a>. The communist alliance drove some deputies to form the rival Indépendants d'Outre-Mer. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> in 1949 the French authorities used local rivalries to justify mass arrests and violent repression; in Dimbokro French troops shot 13 Ivorian civilians, and on 1 February 1950 all RDA meetings in French West Africa were banned. The year left over 50 dead and 3,000 RDA supporters imprisoned.</p><p>Houphouët-Boigny then broke with the communists, made a secret accord with the Indépendants d'Outre-Mer on 9 August 1950, and in December voted for the government of René Pleven, beginning a long partnership with the Democratic and Socialist Union of the Resistance and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a>. In the 1956 elections the RDA became the largest party of the African colonies, and Houphouët-Boigny became the first representative from French West or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">Equatorial Africa</a> to sit in the French cabinet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>As minister, Houphouët-Boigny shaped the 1958 vote that let each colony choose immediate independence or a community with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The question of independence and federalism split the RDA, and the regional party largely dissolved that year, though many national parties kept its name. The RDA parties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> in the Union of African States, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> formed the Conseil de l'Entente. The party opposed colonial rule and held pan-Africanist views without seeking outright secession from France.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rassemblement Démocratique Africain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Keïta and Hamani Diori, led their countries to independence; Léopold Sédar Senghor and Félix Houphouët-Boigny sat in the French parliament and served in governments of the Fourth Republic; and the <b>Rassemblement Démocratique Africain</b>, founded at Bamako in 1946, became the first African mass party organised across several territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
